  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>A <strong>metastasis</strong> is a deposit of tumour whose cells reached the brain with the bloodstream from another organ. In adults it is one of the most common brain tumours.</p>
    <p>Lung cancer, breast cancer, melanoma, kidney and bowel cancer most often spread to the brain. A metastasis keeps the properties of the original tumour: a lung cancer metastasis in the brain is still lung cancer, and it is treated with that in mind.</p>
    <p>On MRI metastases usually look like round lesions with swelling around them. There may be one lesion or several.</p>
    <Scheme />
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>headache, nausea, vomiting;</li>
      <li>weakness or numbness of an arm or leg, problems with speech, vision or coordination;</li>
      <li>seizures;</li>
      <li>changes in behaviour and memory, confusion.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In patients already being treated for cancer, any new neurological symptom is a reason for a brain MRI.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brain MRI with contrast</strong> — the main test: shows the number, size and location of lesions. Small metastases can be missed without contrast.</li>
      <li><strong>Whole-body work-up</strong> (CT, PET-CT and others) — to find the primary tumour and assess the extent of the disease.</li>
      <li><strong>Histology</strong> — after removal of a lesion, if the primary tumour is unknown or the diagnosis needs clarifying.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <p>The strategy is discussed at a <strong>tumour board</strong>: a neurosurgeon, an oncologist and a radiation oncologist. They consider the number and size of lesions, their location, the patient's condition and how well the underlying disease is controlled.</p>
    <h3>Surgery</h3>
    <p>Removal is most often considered if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>there is one lesion or a few, and they are accessible;</li>
      <li>the metastasis is large and causes swelling, brain compression and symptoms;</li>
      <li>the primary tumour is unknown and histology is needed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>I perform the operation microsurgically with neuronavigation — this helps locate the lesion precisely and remove it completely without damaging the surrounding brain.</p>
    <h3>Radiosurgery and radiotherapy</h3>
    <p>Small lesions, including multiple ones, are irradiated precisely with radiosurgery. After removal of a large metastasis its bed is irradiated to reduce the risk of recurrence.</p>
    <h3>Drug treatment</h3>
    <p>Chemotherapy, targeted therapy and immunotherapy are prescribed by an oncologist. Drugs to reduce brain swelling are also prescribed by a doctor.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="posle">
    <h2>After treatment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Brain MRI follow-up — regularly, on the oncologist's schedule: new lesions may appear, and they are best found when small.</li>
      <li>Treatment of the underlying disease continues with the oncologist.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

